            <h2>13. Common Marathi Pronunciation Challenges</h2>
            <p>Even the best AI models sometimes struggle with the unique sounds of regional languages. Understanding these challenges helps you fix them quickly.</p>
            <p><strong>Compound Words (Jodakshar):</strong> Marathi uses many combined consonants. Sometimes the AI might separate them slightly. If this happens, try writing the word phonetically in English letters to see if the engine handles it better.</p>
            <p><strong>Regional Names:</strong> Specific names of local villages or historical figures might be pronounced with a Hindi accent instead of a Marathi one. You can fix this by adding spaces between syllables to force the AI to read it differently.</p>
            <p><strong>Punctuation Issues:</strong> If a sentence feels rushed, the AI lacks breathing room. You must manually add commas where you want a natural pause. If you want a long pause, add two full stops (..).</p>

            <h2>14. Marathi AI Voice vs Human Voice</h2>
            <p>Is AI better than a human? The answer depends entirely on your project goals and resources.</p>
            <p><strong>Cost:</strong> AI is the clear winner here. Tools like Vani AI are free, while human voice actors charge per minute or per project.</p>
            <p><strong>Time:</strong> AI generates audio in seconds. A human needs time to read the script, record, make mistakes, re-record, and edit the final file.</p>
            <p><strong>Consistency:</strong> AI sounds exactly the same every single day. A human voice changes based on their health, the time of day, and the microphone they use.</p>
            <p><strong>Emotion:</strong> Humans win this category. If you need someone to sound genuinely heartbroken or extremely terrified for a drama script, a human actor is still necessary. AI is best for clear, neutral, or mildly enthusiastic narration.</p>

