            <h2>16. Common Mistakes Beginners Make</h2>
            <p>When you start using text-to-speech tools, it is easy to make simple errors that ruin the final audio. Here are 35 specific mistakes and how to avoid them.</p>

            <ul class="compact-list">
                <li><strong>Mistake 1:</strong> Not adding commas. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Add commas where you naturally take a breath when speaking.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 2:</strong> Forgetting full stops at the end of sentences. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: The AI will rush into the next sentence without a full stop. Always include them.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 3:</strong> Using very long paragraphs. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Break text into smaller chunks of 3-4 sentences for better AI processing.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 4:</strong> Using formal textbook language. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Write conversationally. Say things exactly how you would say them to a friend.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 5:</strong> Not testing a short sample first. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Always generate the first paragraph as a test before doing the whole script.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 6:</strong> Ignoring background music. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Pure voice can sound dry. Add very soft, appropriate instrumental music behind the voice.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 7:</strong> Background music is too loud. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Keep music at 10% to 15% volume relative to the voice track.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 8:</strong> Using the wrong voice gender for the topic. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Match the voice profile to the context of the story.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 9:</strong> Not checking auto-translated scripts. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: If you translate English to Marathi, read it manually to fix grammar errors before generating audio.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 10:</strong> Leaving English words spelled in English. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Write English words using Devanagari script (e.g., कॉम्प्युटर) so the Marathi AI reads it correctly.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 11:</strong> Generating too much text at once on limited tools. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Use tools that handle long scripts, or process in 500-word sections.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 12:</strong> Rushing the pacing for educational content. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Slow the voice down slightly if delivering heavy factual information.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 13:</strong> Making the intro too long. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Start the actual content within the first 10 seconds of the audio.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 14:</strong> Repeating the same words too often. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Use synonyms. AI sounds unnatural if it repeats the exact same word pattern closely.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 15:</strong> Forgetting question marks. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: A question mark tells the AI to raise the pitch at the end of the sentence.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 16:</strong> Using complex acronyms. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Write out acronyms fully (e.g., World Health Organization instead of WHO) if the AI stumbles.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 17:</strong> Ignoring volume normalization. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Ensure your final audio export is loud enough for mobile phone speakers.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 18:</strong> Using overly emotional voices for pure news. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Keep news delivery neutral and professional.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 19:</strong> Leaving large gaps of silence in the script. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Edit out unnecessary empty spaces in your video editor.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 20:</strong> Not adding a call to action. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Always ask the listener to subscribe or like at the end of the script.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 21:</strong> Copy-pasting directly from Wikipedia. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Rewrite information in your own words to avoid robotic, dry language.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 22:</strong> Using emojis in the script. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Emojis can confuse the TTS engine. Remove them before pasting.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 23:</strong> Inconsistent voice usage across a channel. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Pick one specific voice profile and use it for all videos to build brand recognition.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 24:</strong> Overcomplicating the video editing. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Focus on clear audio first. Simple visuals are fine if the audio is good.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 25:</strong> Ignoring audience comments about pronunciation. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: If viewers say a word is wrong, learn to spell it phonetically for the next video.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 26:</strong> Expecting instant YouTube success. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Consistency matters. Publish regularly for months.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 27:</strong> Using low-quality background images. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Match high-quality audio with clear, high-resolution visuals.</em></li>
                <li><strong>Mistake 28:</strong> Not structuring the script logically. <br><em class="text-slate-500">Solution: Have a clear beginning, middle, and end.</em></li>
